schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Blockchain
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A digital record (ledger) that many computers (nodes) keep identical,
    synchronized copies of, organized into blocks that each carry a hash
    (fingerprint) of the previous block, making the history tamper-evident
    and removing the need for a single trusted keeper.
  source_traditions:
    - "distributed systems / distributed ledger technology"
    - "cryptography (cryptographic hashing, consensus mechanisms)"
    - "digital currency (Bitcoin's original use case)"
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Introduced via a "village notebook" analogy for readers new to crypto;
    grounds the toolkit's later, more technical articles (proof of
    work/stake, wallets, decentralization) in a plain-language first
    encounter.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "distributed ledger (the general category; a blockchain is one specific structure — hash-chained blocks — within it)"
    - "database with replication (superficially similar copy-everywhere idea, but lacks the hash-chaining + consensus that make a blockchain tamper-evident without a trusted operator)"
  important_distinctions:
    - "immutable ≠ unchangeable-in-theory — rewriting settled history is possible in principle but requires redoing every subsequent block and persuading a majority of nodes simultaneously, which is what makes it impractical rather than impossible"
    - "trustless ≠ untrustworthy — it means no single keeper needs to be trusted because every node can verify every other node's records"
  tensions:
    - "permanence protects against tampering but also means mistakes (wrong entries, misdirected payments) usually can't be undone"
    - "public chains are often fully visible, so transparency and privacy pull against each other for sensitive community information"
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "blockchain" as a single monolithic technology with one energy
    profile or one privacy model — proof-of-work vs. proof-of-stake chains
    differ by roughly three orders of magnitude in energy use, and public
    vs. permissioned chains differ sharply in visibility.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-blockchain.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
